A||||9||whamel||4-6 conversion kit||||1||
Z||000000||whamel||08-24-2000||11:43 AM||||I was thinking of looking at the Kick Hill 4 cylinder to V-6 conversion kit and wondered if anybody had any experience with it.  I've got an '84 with a blown motor that I would really love to get back on the road.  I think that might be the quickest way to do it too.  <BR>Thanks,<BR>Wade||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||08-24-2000||11:56 AM||||That would be a good way if you don't have one in a local salvage yard. You want to tell Kickhill exactly what you have now so they can dig up all the right V6 stuff for you.<P>They'll need to know what tranny, AC, maybe other stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000002||whamel||08-24-2000||12:56 PM||||I think I might go that route - my local junkyard wants a fortune for a 2.8 (strangely enough) that I'd have to rebuild.  In fact, it would almost be cheaper to drive to CT and pick one up from Kick Hill when I bought the kit.<BR>I'd just like it to be a clean swap with little complication since I'll probably be taking the engine out again at some point and replacing it.<BR>Thanks,<BR>Wade||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||08-24-2000||01:07 PM||||Beware--Unless Kick Hill has done something different in the last 6 months, they don't sell a "kit".  What they'll sell you are the parts you'll need.  I'm not trying to discourage you, but I didn't want you to think that you would be getting something like Archies V8 kit.  You'll still need things like motor mounts, dogbone, etc.  And since you're putting this into an '84, remember that the '84 and '85-'88 wiring were different (something like that).||||reg||1
Z||000004||whamel||08-24-2000||01:15 PM||||Thanks for pointing that out Monkeyman!  Those are very good points.  I've got a pretty big stash of Fiero parts and what I didn't get, I don't think I would have trouble replacing in short order.  You are right though as far as I can tell, it's not a step by step kit - just a harness, a computer, and some 'replacement components'.  Might even be easier just to wire it up myself and nab a computer to go with it.  I'll have to price that out too just to see both angles.  <BR>By the way, speaking of wiring - does anybody know of a place that breaks down the wiring of the Fiero VERY well?  I am probably going to have to tear my whole electrical system apart this weekend and I would like to have a roadmap to guide me.  I still don't have the true shop manual yet - is that any better?||||reg||1
Z||000005||whamel||08-24-2000||01:23 PM||||Actually - I take back what I just said, the web site says the kit includes all components you need to do the swap.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||08-24-2000||01:44 PM||||Whamel,<BR>Since no V-6 was available in 84, the swap is supposed to be pretty complicated. They redesigned a bunch of stuff in 85 to accommodate the V-6. I'd research it carefully. <BR>Not trying to discourage you. Just don't expect a "bolt-in & plug 'n' play" swap.<BR>Just my $.02.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO big V-6)<P>When I die, I want to go peacefully, in my sleep, like Grandpa.<BR>Not screaming, like the passengers in his car.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Xtreme Fiero's||08-24-2000||02:08 PM||||I got a quote for Kick hill for my 84 SE w/ AC and a 4 speed, $750 would get me a 2.8L V6(w/ new bearings & lower than 60k miles), V6 ECM, V6 harness, a later larger radiator, Complete V6 exhaust, all the mounts & dog bone. it seems like a good "Kit" to me BUT there are no instructions and there are probably bolts you will need and not have. also if our 84 harness plugs into any thing on the car like Curise control or TCC we could run into some trouble, and don't forget the fuseable links we may have to add a link box like later cars have. I haven't got thid far along yet so i'm not sure the only real problem I see is the Wireing harness if it is seprate form the car. (ECM to engine only) If it joins the car we'll have problems and mabye have to extent the harness.||||reg||1
Z||000008||whamel||08-24-2000||02:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Xtreme Fiero's:<BR><B>I got a quote for Kick hill for my 84 SE w/ AC and a 4 speed, $750 would get me a 2.8L V6(w/ new bearings & lower than 60k miles), V6 ECM, V6 harness, a later larger radiator, Complete V6 exhaust, all the </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>     Let me get that straight - $750 including the engine and the kit??  That's not bad at all - my junkyard wants $800 for just the V6 in poor condition!<BR>     Also - I know it's probably not going to bolt straight in and I'll have wiring harness issues, but at least I have a V6 fiero I can put next to it and make them look the same.  Heck, I'd do it even if I had to assemble and run my own custom harness.  I'll have to put both my cars on stands and do some measuring.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Sage||08-24-2000||02:48 PM||||Don't forget the fuel pump.  It also has to be changed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Good luck, and have a good one!||||reg||1
